  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates an electrically triggered drug-delivery surface, likely a microfabricated implant or patch. At the top, a rectangular device contains a repeating array of small patterned compartments or electrodes distributed across its surface, suggesting multiple individually addressable release sites.\n\nThe enlarged schematic below explains the working principle. In the resting state, a drug reservoir lies beneath a thin patterned top layer that appears to seal the contents in place. After the application of electric pulses, parts of this upper layer open or retract, creating an aperture. Curved arrows rising from the opening indicate that the stored drug is then released upward and outward from the reservoir.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes controlled, on-demand release rather than passive diffusion alone. The key comparison is between the closed configuration, where the drug remains contained, and the electrically activated configuration, where the surface structure changes to permit release. The repeated architecture in the full device suggests that this mechanism can be reproduced across many sites, potentially enabling spatially and temporally precise dosing from a compact materials-based platform.",
